      "side": 1,
      "message": "TL;DR: I think the benefit of weak symbols is bigger than the risk.\n\nI would like to avoid #ifdef ... #endif because that\u0027ll reduce readability especially when they\u0027re nested (as you would see here). Also I think it\u0027s redundant to define CONFIG_I_HAVE_OVERRIDE_X and define X. It\u0027s not difficult to search for a override because in CrOS EC, config is per board. So, overrides should live in the board directory. So, I prefer weak symbols.\n\nOne scenario I\u0027m concerned is the following:\n\n1. A developer finds the default supplier_priority doesn\u0027t work. \n2. They override it to fix a bug but spell it as \u0027suppliers_priority\u0027 by mistake.\n3. Bug is not fixed.\n\nIt\u0027ll waste some time for careless developers but misspelled overrides are unlikely checked in and contaminate the codebase.\n\nIf supplier_priority is renamed, we\u0027ll need to take care of all overrides but that\u0027s rare and not difficult to do.\n\nAnother scenario is a developer chooses exactly the same variable name with exactly the same storage class and type and it breaks neither charge_manager nor the new code being added. It\u0027s possible but it doesn\u0027t seem very likely.",
